  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A tuple of positive integers (a,b,c)(a, b, c)(a,b,c) is said to be a bad tuple if a,b,ca, b, ca,b,c are distinct, aaa divides bbb, and bbb divides ccc. For example, (1,2,4)(1, 2, 4)(1,2,4) and (7,21,42)(7, 21, 42)(7,21,42) are bad tuples while (1,2,3)(1, 2, 3)(1,2,3) and (2,4,4)(2, 4, 4)(2,4,4) are not.\n\nChef has the NNN integers from 111 to NNN with him. He wants to pick a subset of these numbers such that it doesn't contain a bad tuple. How many distinct subsets can he pick?\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first and only line of input contains a single integer NNN, the number of available integers.\n\n\nOutput Format\nOutput the number of subsets that don't contain any bad tuples in them.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤N≤601 \\leq N \\leq 601≤N≤60"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
